(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-decongestants-cough-quiz"]={"title":"Decongestants, Antitussives & Expectorants Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What adverse effects are described with inhaled N-acetylcysteine?","choices":["Sedation and confusion","Hypertension and tachycardia","A rotten egg smell, nausea and vomiting","Nosebleed and septal perforation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The smell comes from its sulfur content and is a real reason patients refuse it; the bronchospasm is the one that matters clinically. Bronchospasm. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which complications of persistent coughing are described as common?","choices":["Stroke, syncope and rib fracture","Pneumothorax, haemoptysis and chest pain","Hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo","Insomnia, exhaustion, pain and hoarseness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unglamorous but genuinely disabling, and together they are the reason cough is treated at all. The pain is musculoskeletal, from the effort of coughing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with pseudoephedrine?","choices":["Fast heart rate","Sedation and dry mouth","Diarrhoea and abdominal pain","Nosebleed and bitter taste"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three follow from stimulating alpha receptors throughout the body rather than only in the nose. Raised blood pressure and headache. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with dextromethorphan?","choices":["Gastric ulceration and bleeding","Confusion, excitement and agitation","Nosebleed and septal perforation","Tachycardia and hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All central, which follows from where it acts and separates it cleanly from benzonatate. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In which patients does dornase alfa give the greatest benefit?","choices":["Those with severe disease only","Those with no symptoms","It benefits all stages equally","Those with mild to moderate disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It still works in severe disease, but the gain in lung function is smaller, which is a point about expectation rather than eligibility. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What does inhaled hypertonic saline improve?","choices":["Bronchial smooth muscle tone","Airway surface hydration","Neutrophil DNA breakdown","Cough receptor sensitivity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It works by changing the conditions the cilia operate in rather than by acting on the mucus chemically. Mucus transport and mucociliary clearance. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the mechanical sequence of a cough once the reflex fires?","choices":["Forced expiration with the glottis held open throughout","Passive exhalation followed by a sharp inspiration","Deep inspiration","Diaphragmatic relaxation followed by chest wall recoil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Closing the glottis against a contracting chest is what builds the pressure, and releasing it is what produces the velocity. Glottic closure, then contraction of chest wall, diaphragm and abdominal wall. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How does dextromethorphan suppress cough?","choices":["It anaesthetises airway stretch receptors","It constricts bronchial vessels","It breaks disulfide bonds in mucus","It suppresses the medullary cough centre"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is related to codeine and acts centrally, which is the origin of both its confusion as a side effect and its misuse. It does so through sigma receptor activation, and it is related to codeine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A patient with a dry cough is taking an antidepressant. Which antitussive is the safer choice, and why?","choices":["Dextromethorphan, because it acts centrally and works faster","Either, since neither interacts with antidepressants","Neither -- an expectorant should be used instead","Benzonatate, because dextromethorphan risks serotonin syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both would suppress the cough; the interaction is what decides between them. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the stated goals of treating a cough?","choices":["To eliminate the cough completely","To treat the underlying infection instead","Fewer, milder episodes and no complications","To prevent transmission to other people"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note that abolishing the cough is not the goal, which matters because a productive cough is doing a job. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which ear complaint is pseudoephedrine indicated for?","choices":["Acute otitis media in a young child","Otitis externa following swimming","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A nasal decongestant given for an ear problem, which makes sense once the tube is understood as opening into the nose. Specifically, the eustachian tube dysfunction that follows a viral infection. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the central instruction for a patient given a topical nasal decongestant?","choices":["Use it every day through the allergy season","Do not use it beyond a few days","Use it only at night","Always combine it with a nasal steroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the whole safety message for this drug, and exceeding it is what produces rebound congestion. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Where is the cough reflex organised?","choices":["In a cough centre in the medulla oblongata","In the cerebral cortex, under voluntary control","In the cervical spinal cord","In the vagal ganglia alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Receptors in the airway start it, but the medulla organises it, which is why one drug class works at each end. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which patient must not be given dextromethorphan?","choices":["One taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor","One taking an oral antihistamine","One with treated hypertension","One with a productive, chesty cough"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The two-week tail is the part that catches people out, because the patient has already stopped the drug. The restriction extends for two weeks after stopping one, which is the part most easily missed. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A patient has used a topical decongestant spray daily for three weeks and is more blocked than when they started. What has happened?","choices":["They have developed allergic rhinitis","Rebound congestion, rhinitis medicamentosa","Bacterial sinusitis has developed","Tolerance, with no rebound effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The drug produces the symptom it was bought to relieve, which is why the patient keeps using it and why the cycle is hard to break. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does guaifenesin do?","choices":["Loosens mucus and reduces its viscosity","Suppresses the cough reflex centrally","Constricts nasal vessels","Blocks histamine receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It makes secretions easier to clear rather than stopping the cough, so it works with the cough rather than against it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why must a benzonatate capsule be swallowed whole?","choices":["It releases local anaesthetic into the mouth","Chewing destroys the active drug before it works","Chewing causes irritation of the stomach lining","Chewing gives the medicine a bitter taste"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It numbs whatever it touches, so releasing it in the mouth numbs the airway a patient needs to protect. And into the throat, which is the airway the patient needs to protect. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How does inhaled N-acetylcysteine reduce mucus viscosity?","choices":["It cleaves DNA from degenerating neutrophils","It splits the disulfide bonds linking mucoproteins","It draws water onto the airway surface","It inhibits mucus secretion at the gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It breaks the chemical cross-links that make mucus stiff, which is a different target again from the DNA that dornase alfa cuts. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"}]};
